<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Germanic paganism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Germanic religion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Germanic paganism was the traditional religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, spanning at least one thousand years across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and at times other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, from the first Roman reports to the conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Few sources were written by its adherents. Its peoples believed in many gods, among them *Wodanaz (Odin), *Thunraz (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Thor</a>), *Tiwaz (Tyr) and *Frijjō (Frigg), and in jötnar, dwarfs, elves and dragons. Archaeology shows rich grave goods, ship and wagon burials, carved wooden figures and sacrificial deposits in bogs and springs, as well as sacred groves and temples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/germanic-paganism.jpg" alt="Germanic paganism" width="330" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bloodofox · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bracteate_from_Funen,_Denmark_(DR_BR42).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term Germanic religion is generally kept for the period after the Germanic languages had become distinct from other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European languages</a>, in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. It was never a unified or codified set of beliefs and showed strong regional variation, so Rudolf Simek writes that it is better to speak of Germanic religions. Celtic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> were in close contact in the first millennium BCE, and Celtic influence appears in religious vocabulary: the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Thor</a> is identical to Celtic Toranos, and the words for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/runes">runes</a> and for sacred groves are of Celtic origin. In contact areas such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, Germanic paganism resembled the religions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnic-peoples">Finnic peoples</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> records that Germanic tribes descended from the gods Tuisto, who was born from the earth, and Mannus, and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semnones">Semnones</a> believed they originated in a sacred grove. A myth of the end of the world is best known as Ragnarök in Old Norse sources, a war between the gods and the beings of chaos ending in fire and a rebirth of the world. Unlike the Christian god, the Germanic gods were born, could die and could not change the fate of the world. They were human in form and family, with superhuman senses and strength, and gave heil, good fortune, for correct observance. Giants were as important as the gods in myths of creation and the end of the world.</p><p>Beliefs about the afterlife varied, among them an underworld, life in the grave, a world of the dead in the sky and reincarnation. The two most important afterlives were Hel and Valhalla. Tacitus named Mercury as the most worshipped god, then Hercules and Mars, which scholars usually read as Odin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Thor</a> and Tyr, although some doubt it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Few sources were written by adherents, and most were written by outsiders, which makes authentic belief hard to reconstruct. Whether the Roman-era reports and Norse paganism of the high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> show continuity is disputed, since most gods named in the Roman period cannot be related to a later Norse god. The far larger body of Scandinavian material has led to a tendency, a methodological problem, to use it to fill gaps in the sparse evidence for continental Germanic religion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>'s Edda is a disputed witness to pre-Christian cosmology, as he imposed an ordered, Christian worldview on his material.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Germanic paganism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
